Rewritten: the plugin's one tool, `storm_run_panel`, as a Nodecode cell. Panel.py's two
prompts are carried nearly word for word — the seat instruction (role, brief, the
questions it must answer from its own viewpoint, ground claims in retrievable sources,
mark what is unsourced, end with 3-5 key findings) and the judge instruction (recommend,
do not decide; consensus first, then contradictions with weights, then what stays
unresolved) — as are the round's shape (every seat on every lineup slot, one judge over
all the reports), the caps (12 seats x 4 slots), its rule that a slot names its own
model and never a model the plugin invented, its refusal to swap in a replacement when a
seat fails, the "success/panel/calls" answer, and the plain-language hint a failed run
gives. Where the plugin called the host's `ctx.llm`, this cell calls `nle:complete`, the
host's own side call, and pins the provider and model through the target that call
resolves — so, as in the original, the cell never reads a key or a credential store. The
plugin's skill (its SKILL.md and reference notes) is carried as the manual `(help :storm)`
plus the tool description, condensed to what a round needs.

Left out, and why: mixture-of-agents presets as a slot and nested fanout (the plugin
reaches them through a host-specific provider id that a Nodecode lane need not have; a
preset's reference models can be listed as separate lineup slots instead); the per-call
`temperature` and the per-call `max_tokens` override as a tool argument (kept, but as the
section's ceiling, since the host's side call takes no temperature); and the token-usage
report alongside each seat (the side call answers text, not usage). The standalone
`run_panel.py` CLI, which posts to an arbitrary OpenAI-compatible endpoint with its own
key, is left out: in Nodecode the lanes and their credentials already exist, and a second
key path would be a second way to fail. The human gates live in the conversation rather
than in a state machine, so a round is one call rather than four checkpoints.
